# tradingview-tester

Research repo for one question: **is there an intraday CL (crude oil) strategy that passes the Topstep 50K Trading
Combine consistently?** CL only: MCL is banned (non-syariah for the owner).

Like a flight simulator for the Combine: replay 16 years of 1-minute CL bars, place the strategy's limit orders under
strict fill rules, then run every possible Combine attempt through Topstep's loss limits and consistency rule.

## Pipeline

```
data/cl1 (Databento CL.v.0 1m)
   │  cltester.data      load bars, add Chicago session fields (trading day, minutes since 17:00 CT)
   ▼
strategies/<name>.py     signals(): one limit bracket order per signal bar
   │  cltester.engine    limit fills on touch, stop/target/flatten 3:10 PM CT/roll exits, fees, MAE
   ▼
trades
   │  cltester.metrics   net, PF, drawdown, years positive
   │  cltester.topstep   50K Combine: EOD-trailing MLL on intraday equity, optional DLL, 55% consistency
   ▼
results/ledger.csv       one row per run, never deleted
```

## Layout

| Path | What |
|---|---|
| `cltester/` | the toolkit: `config` (contract, costs, Topstep rules, periods), `data`, `update`, `engine`, `topstep`, `metrics`, `swings` (owner swing rule), `indicators`, `strategy`, `cli` |
| `strategies/` | one file per strategy; `template.py` is a plumbing check only |
| `docs/` | `PROTOCOL.md` (how research is done), `TOPSTEP.md` (rules + what to verify), `ENGINE.md` (fill rules), `SWINGS.md` (mandatory swing rule), `DATA.md` |
| `docs/strategies/<name>/` | hypothesis and results per strategy (`TEMPLATE.md`) |
| `tests/` | one test per fill rule and Combine rule |
| `data/` | paid bars, local only |
| `web/` | front end (own repo) |

## Use

```bash
.venv/bin/python -m cltester.cli info                       # data range, periods
.venv/bin/python -m cltester.cli update                     # Databento: shows cost, asks first
.venv/bin/python -m cltester.cli test <name> --period dev   # backtest + Combine simulation, logs to ledger
.venv/bin/python -m pytest -q && .venv/bin/ruff check .
```

New strategy: write `docs/strategies/<name>/README.md` from the template first (hypothesis, rules, trial budget),
then `strategies/<name>.py` (see `cltester/strategy.py` for the contract).

## Fixed assumptions

- 1 CL contract unless the strategy sizes up (max 5). Tick $0.01 = $10.
- Fee $4.04 per contract round turn. Entries are limit orders; a touch fills; exits have no slippage.
- Topstep numbers are from 2026 third-party guides; see the VERIFY list in `docs/TOPSTEP.md`.
# Research protocol

Goal: find an intraday CL strategy that passes the Topstep 50K Combine consistently and keeps passing on data it
was not built on. The protocol exists to stop curve-fitting: with enough tries, something always looks good
in-sample.

## Periods (`cltester/config.py`, by CME trading day, end exclusive)

Periods are cut on `trading_day`, never mid-session. Any `test` period that overlaps holdout (`holdout`, `all`)
needs the holdout guard.

| Period | Range | Use |
|---|---|---|
| dev | 2010-06 to 2021-12 | build and tune. Unlimited looks, but every run is logged |
| validation | 2022 to 2024 | frozen parameters, one run per candidate. No tuning after looking |
| holdout | 2025 to 2026-11-12 | once per strategy (the CLI refuses a second run without `--unlock`) |
| forward | after 2026-11-12 | new bars only, then paper/live on TopstepX |

Honesty note: before the 2026-11-12 reset the owner had already researched every year up to 2026. Validation and
holdout are therefore "not used in this repo", not truly unseen. Only **forward** is clean.

## Stages

1. **Hypothesis first.** Before any run, write `docs/strategies/<name>/README.md`: the idea, why CL should behave
   that way, exact entry/exit rules, parameters, the trial budget (how many combinations will be tried) and the pass
   criteria. Template: `docs/strategies/TEMPLATE.md`.
2. **Dev.** `python -m cltester.cli test <name> --period dev`. Tune within the declared budget.
3. **Robustness on dev.** Neighbouring parameters must also be profitable;
   positive in most years; not carried by a few days.
4. **Validation.** Frozen parameters, one run. Record the result whatever it is.
5. **Holdout.** Once. A fail ends the candidate (a new idea is a new strategy, not a retune).
6. **Forward.** New bars and paper trading on TopstepX before real money.

## Proposed gates (owner may change)

| Gate | dev | validation |
|---|---|---|
| Trades | ≥ 200 | ≥ 60 |
| Profit factor (net) | ≥ 1.25 | ≥ 1.10 |
| Years positive | ≥ 70% | — |
| Combine rolling pass rate | ≥ 50% of finished attempts | ≥ 40% |
| Sequential cost per pass | ≤ $500 | ≤ $750 |

## Reporting rules

- Every `test` run appends to `results/ledger.csv` (strategy, code+params hash, period, fills, key stats).
  Never delete ledger rows.
- Every reported number says its period and **in-sample**, and for tuned results **best of N** (N from the ledger).
- Fills follow docs/ENGINE.md (touch fills, no exit slippage). Never call fees "slippage" or slippage "commission".
- A result is not real until the trade list has been spot-checked against raw bars.
# Swing point rule (owner, mandatory)

Every strategy, study or chart that uses swing points uses `cltester/swings.py`. No other pivot method, no
adjustable swing settings. Tests: `tests/test_swings.py`.

## Owner rules (2026-11-12)

| # | Rule |
|---|---|
| 1 | Swing points alternate: low, high, low, high … |
| 2 | Swing low at candle k: left candle low ≥ low[k] (equal allowed) and right candle low > low[k] (strict). |
| 3 | Swing high at candle k: left candle high ≤ high[k] (equal allowed) and right candle high < high[k] (strict). |
| 4 | A swing low is the lowest price of the whole transition between the swing highs around it (every candle counts, not only candidates). Swing high: mirror. Example: SH 70, SL 68, SH 71 or 69: 68 is the lowest price between the two highs. |
| 5 | A second low with no swing high between: lower replaces the first; higher is not a swing low. Swing high: mirror. |
| 6 | A point must meet 1, 2/3, 4 and 5 together. |

## Owner answers to edge cases (2026-11-12)

- **Equal lows** (or equal highs) with no opposite point between: the **later** one is the swing point.
- **A candle can be both** swing low and swing high (outside candle) when both rule 2 and rule 3 hold. Its two
  prices count in the order they printed:
  - higher timeframes: the order of the 1-minute bars inside it (first 1m bar that printed the high vs the low);
  - unknown order (a 1-minute candle, or both prices in the same 1m bar): **continue the order**: after a swing
    high its low comes first, after a swing low its high. If that order would break rule 4 for the existing last
    point, the other order is used (that order is the only one where the structure holds).
- **Rule 4 vs rule 2**: when the lowest candle fails rule 2 because its right candle has an equal or lower low,
  the right candle is the lowest price. When the candle after it has a higher low and a lower high, that right candle
  is the swing low and the swing high (owner example, tested).
- When no candle meets rules 2 and 4 together between two swing highs, there is no swing low between them: the two
  highs merge and rule 5 keeps the higher one.

## Timing (no look-ahead)

- A candidate at candle k is known when candle k+1 **closes**.
- The newest point can still be replaced (rule 5) or removed (rule 4) by later candles. Earlier points can be
  removed only through rule 4 (rare).
- `swing_state(df, tf)`: per 1m bar, the last swing high and swing low **as known at that bar's close**. Use it for
  signals; `prev=True` adds the swing high and low before them (`{tf}_sh2`, `{tf}_sl2`); `newest=True` adds `{tf}_newest`
  (1 swing high, −1 swing low, 0 none): which point came last, also when one candle is both. `swing_points(df, tf)` is the final structure after all data: charts and studies only.
- An unfinished candle is ignored until it is complete, so it can never act as a right candle.
- Every update publishes the full state. A point removed by rule 4 shows as empty (NaN) from that bar on. An old
  level is never carried forward after it was removed.

## Timeframes

`1m, 5m, 15m, 30m, 1h, 4h, 1d, 1w, 1mo`, built from the 1m bars. 5m to 1h follow the clock. 4h blocks start at the
17:00 CT session open. 1d is the CME trading day. 1w is the Monday-to-Friday trading days (Sunday evening belongs to
Monday). 1mo is the trading days of one calendar month (named `1mo`, never `1M`, so it cannot be misread as 1m).
1w and 1mo are complete at the 15:59 CT close of their last trading day (the exchange calendar is known ahead). A candle is complete at the close of its last minute (4h: 20:59, 00:59,
04:59, 08:59, 12:59 CT; the day's last 4h block and the daily candle: 15:59 CT, the last minute before the halt).
If that minute has no bar (early-close holidays, quiet minutes), it is complete at the close of the next bar. Swings
continue across contract changes (prices are not back-adjusted).

## Checked on 2025 data

1m 113,345 points, 5m 24,459, 1h 1,876, 4h 470, 1d 94: zero order breaks, zero rule 2/3 breaks, and no lower low
(higher high) inside any transition. 1m detection takes about 1 s per year.
# Engine fill rules

Code: `cltester/engine.py`. Tests: `tests/test_engine.py` (one test per rule). Change this file with the code.

Owner rules: **entries are limit orders only**, a **touch fills** a limit, and **exits have no slippage**: every
exit fills at the stated price. A gap is not slippage: when the bar opens beyond a stop, the open is the first
traded price, so the stop fills there.

## Order life

| Step | Rule |
|---|---|
| Signal | Computed on bar *i* using data up to bar *i* close. The order works from bar *i+1*. Rows with a missing or non-finite limit/stop are refused. |
| Working | Up to `expire` bars (default 30), or with `expire_min` until that many minutes after the signal bar's close (clock time: missing minutes do not stretch it). Cancelled at the trading-day change, contract change or 3:10 PM CT. |
| One at a time | A signal is ignored while an order works or a position is open. A signal on the exit bar is accepted. |
| Overlap mode | Strategy `OVERLAP = True` (`backtest(..., overlap=True)`): a new signal cancels an order that has not filled (it still works through the new signal's bar); open positions keep running. A signal is ignored when its contracts plus those open at its close would exceed 5 (Combine max); an ignored signal cancels nothing. Contracts that left at a partial exit no longer count after that bar (trades record `part_bar`, `part_qty`). |
| Size | `qty` contracts (default 1); the Combine simulator refuses more than 5. |
| Partial exit | Optional `part_qty` contracts leave at `part_target` (a limit between entry and target); the rest runs on. |
| Daily rules | Optional strategy `day_rules(trades, p)` after the backtest: may only drop the rest of a day once a rule trips (trades are one at a time and flat each day, so that equals stopping live). |
| Management | Optional `manage(ctx)`: on every held bar *m* after the fill bar, before *m*'s exits, using data up to *m*−1 only. Returns the stop for bar *m* and whether to close everything at *m*'s open. |

## Fills (long side; shorts are the mirror image)

| Event | Condition on bar *j* | Fill price |
|---|---|---|
| Limit entry | low ≤ limit (a touch fills) | min(open, limit) |
| Limit entry, strict sensitivity (`--through N`) | low ≤ limit − N ticks (price traded through) | min(open, limit) |
| Void (optional `void` price, at or beyond the limit) | open beyond void: cancelled. Otherwise price reaches the fill level before the void (it lies between), so a bar that reaches both fills; once filled the void no longer matters. Only when the void break comes first (strict fills with the fill level at or past the void break) is the order cancelled; the same price counts as cancelled. A cancelled order still worked on the bar that cancelled it, so a new signal is accepted only from that bar's close | — |
| Kill (optional `kill` price, beyond the limit on the winning side, e.g. the target) | high ≥ kill cancels the unfilled order. An open at or through the fill level fills first (the kill is touched later). Otherwise a bar that reaches both the fill level and the kill: order unknown, cancelled, no fill. With `--through N` the fill level is limit − N ticks. A cancelled order worked on that bar, so a new signal is accepted from its close | — |
| Stop on the fill bar | low of the fill bar ≤ stop (assume the low came after the fill) | min(entry, stop) |
| Flatten | first bar at or after 3:10 PM CT | open |
| Stop at the open | open ≤ stop | open (gap) |
| Target at the open | open ≥ target | open (gap in our favour) |
| Stop | low ≤ stop | stop |
| Partial at the open | open ≥ part_target | open |
| Partial (limit) | high ≥ part_target and the stop is not hit in that bar | part_target |
| Target (limit) | high ≥ target (a touch fills) | target |
| Manage close | `manage` returns close_now (after the flatten check) | open |
| Stop and target both inside one bar | stop first (worst case) | stop |
| Stop and partial both inside one bar | stop first, no partial (worst case) | stop, all contracts |
| Target on the fill bar | not allowed (unknown order inside the bar) | — |
| Time exit (`hold`) | `hold` bars after the fill bar | close |
| Contract change | last bar before `raw_symbol` changes | close |
| Day end | last bar of the trading day (early-close holidays end before 3:10 PM) | close |
| End of data | last bar loaded, position still open | close |

Check order per bar after the fill bar: `manage` (sets the stop), flatten, manage close, stop at open, target at
open, partial at open, stop inside the bar, partial inside the bar, target inside the bar, then end of data / time /
roll / day end at the close. A stop moved by `manage` to or beyond the open exits at the open. `manage` must
return whole ticks in side space (negated for shorts, `ctx.to_side`); a fraction or NaN raises. `ctx.best` starts at the fill bar's
high, which may have printed before the fill. The open is the first price of the bar, so an exit
there happens before anything else in that bar.

Strict fills are a sensitivity check, not the owner rule: a touch-only fill may not happen live (queue position).
`--through` must be 0 or more (negative values are refused). `cli test --through 1` records `"fills": "through1"` in the ledger params and gets its own hash and trade file.

## Money

- CL: tick $0.01 = $10 per contract. Prices are compared as integer ticks.
- Fee $4.04 per contract round turn, charged in `net`, `mae` and `mfe`.
- With a partial exit, `net` adds both legs; `ticks` is the final leg per contract, `part_exit` the partial price.
  The worst P&L of a bar with a partial inside it counts the bar low with every contract held at the bar's open
  (order unknown, conservative); after a partial at the open, only the contracts left. `mfe` counts the partial
  leg at its exit price.
- `mae`: worst open P&L during the trade from bar lows (longs) incl. fee, used by the Combine MLL check.
  An exit at the open counts only the open; a stop inside the bar counts the stop; other exit bars count the whole bar.
- `path`: per held bar, (open P&L, worst P&L) incl. fee. The Combine simulator walks it so a gap through a loss
  limit executes at the open price. Not written to the trade CSV.
  The exit price always counts, so MAE is never better than the realized result.

## Why these rules

- Touch fills and no exit slippage are owner rules. Both are optimistic: in live trading a limit at the touch can
  sit in the queue unfilled, and a stop can fill a tick or more worse. Keep that in mind when a result depends on
  many small wins at exact limit prices.
- Worst-case intrabar order: 1-minute bars do not show the order of high and low. Assuming stop first can only make
  results worse, never better.
- Flat by 3:10 PM CT and at each contract change: Topstep closes positions at 3:10 PM CT, and the continuous series
  is not back-adjusted, so holding through a contract change would book a fake price jump.

